{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact head 2b4b7b2fd4cd7f79a5f3f38dcda953b3116e0f64 (merge base with main 978b8223aa5a736545b3d5cdc57fa415f0de8ca7). One blocking defect, narrow and cheap to fix. Everything else — including the parts I expected to be hardest — passed, and I verified the real-log demonstration independently rather than taking the report's word for it.\n\nDEFECT (blocking): the seam this change exists to define is not pinned by any test. In staleness() the propagation term is (world[basis] \u0026\u0026 artifactProvenance) with artifactProvenance := f.isArtifact(basis) \u0026\u0026 f.isArtifact(record.record.ID). The f.isArtifact(basis) conjunct is uncovered. I mutated the declaration to artifactProvenance := f.isArtifact(record.record.ID) — letting world staleness cross a reasoning-to-artifact edge — and the whole package still passes: \"ok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/workroom 0.200s\". The mutant is not equivalent. Folded over the live log at depth 5515 it marks 149 additional live artifacts as describing a superseded world, including cmd/gs, internal/app and internal/service: this head narrows world-stale artifacts 704 -\u003e 294, the surviving mutant narrows only to 443, so roughly half the churn this change exists to remove would come back with a green suite. The two new fixtures miss it because both place two reasoning hops between the retired artifact and the answering artifact (approval -\u003e follow-up -\u003e follow-up-promise -\u003e answer), so the flag is already dead before an artifact is reached; the uncovered case is one hop — an artifact resting directly on a report/request/promise that is itself world-stale by the seed edge. Requested change: one fold test where an artifact rests directly on such a report, asserting stale=true and describes_superseded_world=false. That kills the mutant and pins \"STOP at request/promise/report edges\" as an edge rule rather than a two-hop coincidence. The request's own words are the argument for it: the fixtures are what let the gap survive.\n\nNON-BLOCKING, worth folding into the same head: AGENTS.md compresses the rule to \"The narrower describes_superseded_world fact follows artifact provenance\", which omits the direct retired-artifact edge — the very edge that makes an approval report refusable at the merge gate, and so the main practical case an agent meets. fold.go and docs/reference/architecture.md state it exactly (\"crosses direct retired-artifact edges and artifact-to-artifact provenance only\"); SKILL.md is also accurate. AGENTS.md should match them.\n\nFOCUS 1, REPLAY DETERMINISM — NO DECISION FLIPS, PROVEN. The change is unconditional: no schema bump, no record flag, no depth cutover. That is correct here, and the ratified proposal says why (\"no schema field is added\"). Structural proof: decisions are made streaming in foldState.append and stored in f.decisions; staleness() is called exactly once, from project(), and project() only copies f.decisions into the projection. No identifier stale or world appears anywhere in the decision region (fold.go 322-1208) — not in decideState, decideRatify, decideSupersede, validateBasis, hasAuthorizedMergeReceipt, validateMergeReceiptNow, mergeReceiptPlan or the fold's own reviewedPaths. So the old fold never refused an act because of staleness, and neither does this one. Empirical proof on the live log, both binaries built from source and run over the identical record set (head ee5abb92fc5ab5ba84cc96900fe9ae84474064ea, depth 5515 in both runs): all 5515 decisions byte-identical on event, verdict and reason; the ordinary stale field byte-identical on all 3751 statements. Only describes_superseded_world moves, and strictly one way — statements 2867 -\u003e 1185 (1682 true-\u003efalse, 0 false-\u003etrue), artifacts 704 -\u003e 294 (410 true-\u003efalse, 0 false-\u003etrue). The narrowing is monotone by construction (the seed term is unchanged and the propagation term only gains a conjunct), which matches the measurement exactly. Projection metadata changing on replay is safe precisely because nothing downstream of it is a decision.\n\nFOCUS 2, DEPLOY ORDER — no footgun, unlike workroom/state@1, and nothing needs to be said in the head. Both mixed states fail closed. Old resident with new gs: the resident computes the wider world set, so gs refuses more than intended — over-refusal, never under-refusal. New resident with old gs: old gs gates on Stale, which this change leaves untouched and which is a superset of world in both versions. The new merge receipt body keys (stale, staleness) cannot be refused by an older fold either: validateFields only walks the constraints a kind declares and ignores unknown keys, so no state@1-style \"old fold refuses new records\" hazard exists. The one practical consequence is that the fix is inert until the resident is restarted on the new binary — a no-op, not a hazard.\n\nFOCUS 3, MERGE-GATE NARROWING, ADVERSARIALLY. Refusals that remain: not effective; retired; verdict not approved; approved head not equal to candidate; approval not resting on its named artifact; artifact commit not equal to candidate; no projected review; self-review; indeterminate independence; approval already spent (both the git receipt and the durable receipt); signer not the approved implementer; and unreachable cross-author retirements. I could not construct an approval that merges while its artifact genuinely describes a superseded world. The gate is computed on the edges that carry that meaning: the approval is required to rest on its named artifact, a retired artifact directly under either the approval or the artifact sets the flag through the seed term regardless of the dependent's kind, and artifact-to-artifact chains keep relaying it, so both the direct and the multi-hop description cases are caught. What genuinely relaxes is an approval whose own review request or promise went stale — a retired-and-refiled review request, say. The old gate refused it; this one merges and seals the reason. That is the ratified tradeoff and the evidence survives in the receipt (stale: true plus a bounded staleness note naming the moved parts and the retired bases). The implementation-side request/promise/report chain never sat under the approval, so nothing there changed. Authority is not widened either: the fold's own reviewedPaths, which is what actually confers cross-author retirement, ignores staleness entirely and always did; the CLI filter in succession.go moves from Stale to DescribesSupersededWorld and so moves closer to the fold's ceiling without ever passing it, and the other four conditions (cited by the approval, non-retired, commit equal to the reviewed head, authored by the implementer) are untouched. Live-log effect on the 13 approved, ratified, non-retired reviews: the old gate would refuse 11 of them, the new gate refuses 0, and exactly one of those heads (7a9e94db9a, the internal/kernel line) is not yet in main and becomes mergeable — the deadlock this change was raised to end. Stated honestly: on today's log the new gate blocks nothing, so the retained refusal is evidenced by the fixture tests and by the still-world-stale docs/reference/mcp/state.md artifact rather than by a currently blocked approval.\n\nFOCUS 4, BOTH DIRECTIONS. Reproduced on the real log, not taken from the report. Kernel artifact ...630998138a58944c562e984fa2964419dc6e5553 at internal/kernel: stale stays true, describes_superseded_world true -\u003e false, so the reasoning line is cut. Documentation artifact ...ef13237b02643f124941146e26379095984c2d85 at docs/reference/mcp/state.md: stale true and describes_superseded_world stays true, so a document resting on behaviour still flares when that behaviour is superseded. Both match the report's claims exactly. Mutation testing of the seam: six fold mutants, five caught — artifactProvenance := true (reverts to the old wide rule) fails both new tests; := false (seed only, no multi-hop) fails the artifact-provenance hop; := isArtifact(basis) || isArtifact(dependent) and := isArtifact(basis) both fail the reasoning-hop assertion; narrowing the seed term to require an artifact dependent fails \"verdict directly naming retired artifact\". The sixth is the defect above. Two merge-gate mutants, both caught: reverting liveArtifact/liveStatement to Stale fails TestMergeGuardRecordsAndMergesAReasoningStaleApproval and TestMergeLivenessSeparatesReasoningStaleFromSupersededWorld; disabling the world check fails TestMergeGuardStillRefusesAMovedWorldAfterAnApprovedReview. So the gate is pinned in both directions; only the fold's propagation edge is half-pinned.\n\nFOCUS 5, PROSE. The contract change is recorded in the same head: docs/reference/architecture.md states both the narrowed propagation rule and the receipt behaviour, and the docs candidate stands at this head, so step 4 is satisfied. No live artifact sits at docs/reference/architecture.md, so docs is the right granularity and no predecessor is stranded. SKILL.md discipline 8 and the AGENTS.md loop both state the narrower rule and both say a world-stale merge needs re-anchoring rather than re-review, as the request required. I also ran the rewritten AGENTS.md migration jq against the live projection: it is well formed and returns 3 heads, and its indirection through statements is necessary and correct, because projection.reviews carries stale but not describes_superseded_world while every artifact event also appears in statements.\n\nARCHITECTURE: affected layers are the Workroom interpreter (internal/workroom, the pure fold) and the cmd/gs composition over it (merge gate, succession planning, receipts), with the repository guidance pages. This is a contract change to the staleness rule, and the head changes it and documents it in docs/reference/architecture.md at the same head, so the contract is preserved as stated rather than silently. The layering itself is untouched: the fold stays pure over records and still verifies no merge head, diff or tree, and the world-staleness check remains a CLI-side judgement over a folded projection, which is where the comment correctly says it has to live.\n\nSECURITY: no defect found. Authority boundaries are unchanged — the fold's reviewedPaths, which is the record-level gate on cross-author retirement, never consulted staleness, so nothing this change touches widens who may retire whose artifacts; the CLI filter only relaxes toward that unchanged ceiling. Signature, ratification and independence checks are untouched, and the self-review and approved-implementer refusals both still fire. Untrusted input: the new staleness note is derived from the projection, not from user text, and is bounded by the pre-existing stalenessCauseCap of 4 plus an \"and N more\" suffix, so an attacker who retires many bases cannot inflate a receipt without limit. The new body keys cannot be used to smuggle force, since the fold reads merge_approval and the plan fields and ignores the rest. The residual risk is the accepted one: an approval whose review request or promise moved can now land. It is bounded by the exact-immutable-head checks, recorded in both the git receipt and the durable assertion, and it is what the ratifier decided.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: this is already the small version of the change, and I found nothing to cut. Two lines of fold, a reuse of the existing Stale/DescribesSupersededWorld pair rather than a new field, and a reuse of the existing bounded reviewStaleness formatter and reviewPart shape for the merge path rather than a second formatter. validateMerge returning (string, error) instead of error is the minimum plumbing needed to seal the reason into the receipt. No new schema, no new rests_on edge type, no temporal bypass. The one addition I am asking for is a test, not code.\n\nGATES, verbatim, run at this exact head in a clean throwaway worktree, no concurrent go test on the machine. go build ./... -\u003e BUILD OK. go vet ./... -\u003e VET OK. go test ./... -count=1 -\u003e all 25 packages ok, exit code 0: gitseq 0.269s; cmd/gitseq-github 0.275s; cmd/gitseq-mcp 64.282s; cmd/gitseq-perf 0.414s; cmd/gs 173.626s; internal/app 62.948s; internal/connector/github 0.968s; internal/custody 4.718s; internal/docset 115.864s; internal/domain 1.807s; internal/gitstore 4.172s; internal/intent 2.799s; internal/kernel 144.268s; internal/nexus 2.739s; internal/observe 1.760s; internal/perflane 2.164s; internal/perfscenario 1.991s; internal/residentclient 2.479s; internal/service 24.353s; internal/statusview 3.356s; internal/telemetry 3.053s; internal/wireparity 2.518s; internal/workroom 1.891s; spike/cmd/gitseq-report 1.989s; spike/cmd/gitseq-spike 3.452s.\n\nNote for whoever routes this: two live review requests now name this head — 9c7f8604a071c85c595df36084212781cc55be6c, which I promised and am reporting against, and a6a5adb831bb. Only one review is needed; the second should be retired or answered deliberately rather than drawing a duplicate verdict.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:bb2986a22fd8d8f794ad474720df424ec5d24d8c","head":"2b4b7b2fd4cd7f79a5f3f38dcda953b3116e0f64","verdict":"changes-requested"}}